{"about":"0b14385d18dbb54744c46bd88d961c2cf563cc0e20d78e2419a4362748cd7e4b","body":"The conjecture accepts the cost that science offers no assurance of future success, but this renders the concept of 'rational choice' vacuous in a pragmatic context. If a scientist cannot claim that a better-corroborated theory is more likely to yield correct predictions than a worse-corroborated one, then the directive to 'choose the best-criticized' provides no actual guidance for action distinct from flipping a coin, thereby undermining the claim that this preference is defensible as a rational strategy rather than a ritualistic adherence to past forms.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The cost of abandoning assurance of future success"}